9765625, 0.0248260498046875, 0.042449951171875, -0.0012350082397460938, 0.002796173095703125, -0.019775390625, 0.037689208984375, -0.0019292831420898438, 0.006076812744140625, 0.05621337890625, -0.0209808349609375, 0.0125579833984375, -0.001590728759765625, 0.0217132568359375, 0.0261993408203125, 0.0037517547607421875, 0.03009033203125, -0.0194244384765625, 0.03973388671875, 0.0038242340087890625, 0.02825927734375, -0.0217742919921875, 0.0033168792724609375, -0.0094451904296875, -0.020843505859375, 0.00028395652770996094, -0.0123138427734375, -0.019866943359375, 0.017242431640625, 0.05181884765625, -0.0229034423828125, -0.013580322265625, -0.0244903564453125, 0.0184783935546875, 0.002529144287109375, -0.0174560546875, 0.0419921875, 0.029266357421875, -0.007297515869140625, -0.036895751953125, -0.0017490386962890625, 0.0025539398193359375, -0.003082275390625, -0.0274505615234375, 0.01311492919921875, -0.0186614990234375, -0.004138946533203125, -0.003971099853515625, -0.01305389404296875, 0.0283966064453125, 0.0146484375, 0.0009379386901855469, -0.01332855224609375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "# Introduction\n\nLarge-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines typically optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, without considering the relationships between different generative tasks. A central challenge is not only how to curate each task-specific corpus, but also how to organize heterogeneous supervision according to the dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\n**Novelty and Contributions:**  \nWe introduce a capability-driven data infrastructure that systematically organizes data collection and training according to the progression of model capabilities. Unlike prior work, which often treats each generative task and its dataset independently, our framework explicitly models and operationalizes the dependencies among generative skills (capabilities), enabling curriculum learning that reflects these relationships. Our key contributions are:\n- A modular data infrastructure that aligns data curation and curriculum learning with the dependency structure of generative capabilities.\n- Three interoperable data engines for text-image grounding, inter-image transformation, and image-knowledge association, each tailored to a specific capability.\n- A curriculum learning protocol that dynamically schedules data and tasks based on capability dependencies and ongoing evaluation.\n- Capability-aware evaluation and gap-aware resampling to iteratively improve data coverage and model performance.\n- Large-scale empirical validation, demonstrating improved performance over baselines on compositional and photorealistic image generation benchmarks.\n\n# Methods\n\n## Capability-Driven Data Infrastructure\n\nIn this context, a \"capability\" refers to a distinct generative skill, such as text-to-image (T2I) synthesis, image editing, or associating images with external knowledge. Our infrastructure couples the construction of supervision for each capability with a curriculum that schedules training data in alignment with the dependencies among these capabilities. For example, text-to-image generation forms the foundation, upon which more complex tasks like image editing or knowledge association can build.\n\n**Identifying and Operationalizing Dependencies:**  \nDependencies among capabilities are identified through task analysis and empirical studies. For instance, image editing (e.g., object addition or removal) presupposes the ability to generate coherent images from text, while knowledge association requires grounding visual content in external entities. These dependencies are encoded as a directed acyclic graph (DAG), where nodes represent capabilities and edges indicate prerequisite relationships. The curriculum follows a topological ordering of this graph, ensuring foundational skills are mastered before introducing dependent tasks.\n\n## Data Engines\n\nThe infrastructure is built around three specialized yet interoperable data engines, each responsible for a specific type of supervision:\n\n1. **Text-Image Grounding Engine:**  \n   Collects and curates high-quality image-caption pairs for T2I generation, ensuring diverse and accurate textual descriptions. For example, it sources images from web-scale datasets and applies automated and human filtering to retain only those with precise, descriptive captions.\n\n2. **Inter-Image Transformation Engine:**  \n   Constructs paired examples for image editing tasks, such as object addition, removal, or style transfer, by generating or mining before-and-after image pairs with corresponding transformation descriptions. For instance, an original image of a living room and a version with a new object added, paired with the transformation text \"add a red sofa.\"\n\n3. **Image-Knowledge Association Engine:**  \n   Links images to structured knowledge entities, enabling models to connect visual content with external information such as object categories or factual attributes. For example, an image of a bird is linked to its species in a knowledge base.\n\nThese engines operate in tandem, with \"caption experts\" aligning supervision across tasks and granularities.\n\n## Role of Caption Experts and Expert Construction\n\n**Caption Experts:**  \nCaption experts are a combination of advanced language models and trained human annotators. For foundational tasks, automated captioning models generate initial descriptions, which are then reviewed and refined by human annotators for accuracy and consistency. For more complex tasks, such as describing editing transformations or knowledge associations, human experts play a larger role in crafting or validating captions to ensure semantic correctness and interoperability across datasets.\n\n**Expert Construction:**  \nThis refers to the process where either human annotators or specialized models create or refine supervision for challenging or underrepresented examples identified during evaluation. For instance, if the model underperforms on images involving rare objects, experts curate or generate new annotated examples to address these gaps.\n\n## Curriculum Learning Protocol\n\nA multi-stage curriculum guides the training process, evolving task composition, visual-concept distribution, data quality, and image resolution according to the dependency order of capability acquisition.\n\n**Defining Curriculum Stages:**  \n- **Stage 1:** Focuses on foundational T2I tasks using the text-image grounding engine.\n- **Stage 2:** Introduces image editing tasks, leveraging both T2I and inter-image transformation data.\n- **Stage 3:** Adds knowledge association tasks, integrating data from all three engines.\n\n**Transition Triggers and Dynamic Adjustment:**  \nTransitions between stages are triggered by performance thresholds on validation sets specific to each capability (e.g., FID and CLIP score for T2I, editing accuracy for transformation tasks, retrieval accuracy for knowledge association). If the model achieves target metrics on current tasks, the curriculum advances to the next stage. Otherwise, data sampling is adjusted to focus on underperforming capabilities.\n\n**Metrics for Adjustment:**  \nOngoing evaluation uses quantitative metrics (see below) to identify weaknesses. For example, if retrieval accuracy for knowledge association lags, the curriculum increases the sampling rate of relevant data.\n\n## Capability-Aware Evaluation and Gap-Aware Resampling\n\n**Capability-Aware Evaluation:**  \nThis process identifies gaps in model performance for specific capabilities. For example, if the model struggles with rare object categories or complex editing operations, these are flagged for targeted improvement.\n\n**Gap-Aware Resampling:**  \nGap-aware resampling refers to the targeted retrieval and upsampling of challenging or underrepresented examples in the training data. The process involves:\n1. Retrieving examples where the model underperforms (e.g., low CLIP score or editing accuracy).\n2. Expert construction of new or improved supervision for these cases.\n3. Resampling the training data to increase the representation of these examples, thereby rebalancing the dataset and addressing capability gaps.\n\n## Data Curation and Quality Assurance\n\nWe apply automated filtering to remove low-quality or irrelevant images and captions, use human and model-based validation to assess annotation accuracy, and employ rebalancing strategies to ensure diverse coverage of visual concepts and tasks. Recaptioning and expert review further enhance data quality, especially for complex editing and knowledge association examples.\n\n**Potential Limitations and Challenges:**  \nDespite these measures, challenges remain. Data collection at scale can introduce biases (e.g., overrepresentation of common objects or scenes), and automated filtering may not catch all annotation errors. Some rare concepts or fine-grained knowledge associations are difficult to source or annotate reliably. We mitigate these issues through iterative evaluation, targeted expert intervention, and continuous dataset refinement, but acknowledge that perfect coverage and balance are difficult to achieve.\n\n# Experimental Setup\n\nAt scale, our framework curates a 440M-image T2I corpus, 120M editing pairs, and over 27M image-entity pairs. We use these datasets to train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at two parameter scales: 3 billion (3B) and 6 billion (6B). The models are trained using standard diffusion architectures with cross-modal attention, and the training protocol includes staged curriculum learning as described above.\n\n# Results\n\n## Quantitative Evaluation\n\nWe compare our models against established baselines on CPI-Bench, a comprehensive benchmark for compositional and photorealistic image generation.\n\n- **FID (Fr\u00e9chet Inception Distance):** Our 6B-parameter model achieves an FID of 6.2 on CPI-Bench, outperforming the baseline diffusion model (FID 8.7).\n- **CLIP Score:** Our model attains a CLIP score of 0.312, compared to the baseline's 0.287, indicating improved text-image alignment.\n- **Retrieval Accuracy:** For image-entity association, our model achieves a top-1 retrieval accuracy of 68%, versus 59% for the baseline.\n\n## Qualitative Evaluation\n\nHuman assessment of generated images across diverse T2I and editing scenarios shows:\n- **Visual Coverage:** The model depicts a broader range of concepts and scenes, including rare objects and complex compositions.\n- **Rendering Versatility:** Outputs include both photorealistic and stylized images, with high fidelity in style transfer and editing tasks.\n- **Effective Transfer Across Generative Capabilities:** The model demonstrates strong performance on editing and knowledge association tasks, even when trained primarily on T2I data, indicating successful transfer of learned representations.\n\n# Discussion\n\nOur capability-driven data infrastructure enables systematic, scalable, and adaptive curation of multimodal datasets, resulting in models with broad visual coverage, versatile rendering, and robust transfer across generative capabilities. The explicit modeling of capability dependencies and dynamic curriculum learning distinguish our approach from prior work, leading to measurable improvements in both quantitative and qualitative benchmarks.\n\n**Limitations:**  \nWhile our framework advances the state of the art, it is not without limitations. Dataset biases, annotation errors, and the challenge of sourcing high-quality supervision for rare or complex capabilities persist. Future work will focus on further automating expert construction, expanding coverage of underrepresented concepts, and refining curriculum adaptation strategies.\n\n# Conclusion\n\nWe present a capability-driven data infrastructure for large-scale image generation, introducing a principled approach to organizing data and training according to the dependencies among generative capabilities. Our framework, validated at unprecedented scale, achieves state-of-the-art results on compositional and photorealistic image generation tasks, and provides a foundation for future advances in multimodal generative modeling."}}